WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.212
A limited view can reduce the columns and rows exposed through that interface.

00:00:05.212 --> 00:00:09.557
That is useful. But it does not by itself guarantee that users cannot access the base table.

00:00:09.557 --> 00:00:14.967
It does not replace grants, row-level security, or broader compliance controls.

00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:18.772
Use precise language about what the view actually protects.

00:00:18.772 --> 00:00:22.632
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:22.632 --> 00:00:27.282
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:27.282 --> 00:00:30.637
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:30.637 --> 00:00:34.597
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:34.597 --> 00:00:40.657
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:46.322
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:00:46.322 --> 00:00:48.400
not only whether the statement executes.
